welcome to the show I am going to talk a  bunch today about what's going on in  Venezuela and talking about this issue  at all is sort of a Pyrrhic victory  because there's a contingent of the left  that is getting Venezuela just very  wrong and when I talk about it I feel  like I'm doing the right thing in the  sense that it's important to point out  when the left goes wrong but these  stories get relatively little traffic  and they do anger a bunch of people in  the audience and they even sometimes get  people to cancel memberships because  people get mad at me over what what they  see as me getting the story's wrong but  it just would not feel right to ignore  the Venezuela story right now ignoring  it would be a missed opportunity to talk  about some really serious geopolitical  issues even if there are people in my  core audience that just flat-out  disagree with me I know every time I  talk about this some people do actually  say you know what there is a bunch of  nonsense going on with this issue on the  left and some people actually do come  around and change their mind so I think  that in the long term it is worth it for  me to keep talking about this and that's  why I'm just gonna jump right into it  today we had this questionably valid  re-election of Venezuelan president like  Nicolas Maduro  and after that the General Assembly  leader Huang hua though was established  as interim president with the idea as  provided by the Venezuelan Constitution  that if there is an election that is  perceived to be invalid the General  Assembly can appoint an interim  president and that interim president is  to oversee elections that are ideally  more fair or fair compared to the ones  that started the entire problem to begin  with I said when we started talking  about this a week or 10 days ago that  what ultimately happens is going to be  hugely determined by whether the  military stays with Nicolas Maduro or  abandons Maduro and we actually have  some news about that and I'm going to  talk about that a little bit later but  what I want to talk about first here is  flat-out how there are a lot of people  on the Left that are just missing the  boat on the foundations of this story  there  are too many leftists in the United  States and in some other countries who  are supporting Nicolas Maduro but for  different reasons some on the Left are  supporting Maduro because they've fallen  for his foe progressive rhetoric this is  sort of like the most romantic reason to  support Nicolas Maduro and many of them  also before Maduro were were equally  taken with Hugo Chavez both of whom  spouted very progressive sounding sort  of utopian socialistic stuff but the  truth is that they are authoritarians  who subjugate the basic democratic  institutions elections the press the  judiciary and others for their own gain  they are running kleptocracy Zinn effect  that's one piece of the left that  supports Maduro another piece of the  left that supports Maduro does it  because they believe to differing  degrees that the Venezuelan opposition  to Chavez and Maduro has from the  beginning bin American neocon funded  astroturf may be tied in with getting  oil from Venezuela now we could do an  entire hour just on that which I know  most of the audience doesn't want  although maybe I'll do a live stream in  more detail about just this issue but  here is why the left is misguided on  this and really needs to get it together  the right is already using Venezuelan  so-called socialism to attack the left  okay to attack actual socialism to  attack social democracy these are bad  faith criticisms from the right and  James Bloodworth points this out really  well in his latest piece for foreign  policy which you should read so the  right is of course going after the  Maduro regime in a way that is  advantageous to their worldview so that  then what happens is when the left  simply defends the regime we end up  losing even bigger because the left is  making the mistake of believing that  they are simply opposing American  imperialism or they're simply supporting  these utopian socialistic ideas by  standing by Nicolas Maduro  the regime but what they are doing in  effect no matter what they envision  themselves to be doing is defending a  brutal authoritarian even if you say  they're not actually defending Maduro  it's that they're just so opposed to  American imperialism or two wars for oil  or whatever the modern left still loses  when we defend Nicolas Maduro and this  is where we get to that term that I've  been using for a little while now the  Leninist left there is an American left  that is focused on anti-imperialism  particularly against the US Empire and  they end up supporting all sorts of de  facto dictators us-led regime change  around the world and in particular in  Latin America has been a disaster it's a  huge red flag when we see the American  neocon establishment want to get  involved in that not in America I  completely agree with you American  military involvement in replacing Maduro  in venezuela could be absolutely  disastrous but does that mean that the  american left should again take the side  of the people that are effectively the  domestic imperialists the Maduro chavez  regime nicolas maduro  domestically is doing many of the things  that the Leninist Left claims to be  opposed to the u.s. doing around the  world i can't support that and i hope  that you don't either even if there are  other countries supporting opposition  groups in Venezuela that doesn't change  that in Venezuela there are tons of  people who oppose the destructive Maduro  regime who are protesting for real a  huge risk to their lives  now the left will come in and say well  behold on its an american-led coup hold  on a second  Maduro has destroyed fair elections  Maduro has eliminated all sorts of  checks and balances you've got elements  of the left doing the exact same thing  with Assad in Syria and then they will  come in and say it's all for oil because  Trump simply wants their oil Trump may  want their oil I quite frankly don't  think Trump really understands much  about the situation there but the oil  companies aren't hugely into  American military intervention in  Venezuela they're pretty happy with the  status quo getting to refine the low  quality crude oil from Venezuela making  money off of it so the idea that there's  the sort of United Front of the United  States government and oil companies to  take Venezuelan oil and upend the the  status quo that's simply not true  you can agree or disagree with one or  both but it's not a cut and dry position  that the US government and oil companies  have a particular plan that they're all  in agreement with for Venezuelan oil so  that's also not a good argument so where  I end up is the same place as a week ago  which is that I'm a progressive I do not  support any of these authoritarians I at  the same time I'm against American  neoconservative interventions and I am  afraid that Trump will get involved that  doesn't mean that I support either  implicitly or explicitly Nicolas Maduro  Maduro and Chavez before him were  kleptocrats  just like Trump who governed or  themselves except that Maduro and Chavez  did and do enough redistribution and  rhetoric that sounds populist that  allows them to keep some popularity and  retain power it's not for me let's  suppose leaders who should be opposed  and let's vote for people in the United  States who won't do the types of  interventions in Latin America that  we're against we don't need to stand by  Maduro because we think maybe Trump is  interested in Venezuelan oil
 

